<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
easl under the hardened runtime (scripts/notarize.sh signs with it; docs/releasing.md).

easl itself needs no runtime exception: libghostty and the tree-sitter grammars are linked in;
Metal, WebKit (its JIT runs in the WebContent process), the JavaScriptCore parser behind
browser.eval (parses, never runs), UserNotifications, the sockets and every subprocess it spawns
(zmx, shells, git, language servers, python3, bun) work without one. Subprocesses don't inherit
these entitlements.

What they do inherit is TCC responsibility: macOS attributes a privacy request from anything
running in a terminal tile, and from a browser tile's page, to easl, and under the hardened
runtime tccd refuses to even ask the user unless easl carries the matching entitlement ("Prompting
policy for hardened runtime; service: kTCCServiceMicrophone requires entitlement
com.apple.security.device.audio-input but it is missing for responsible=net.waldin.easl").
Each key below only lets macOS ask; the user still decides, and Info.plist says why (bundle.sh).
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- osascript and other AppleScript automation run by agents or users in a terminal tile. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- Voice input in terminal programs (agent dictation, sox, whisper) and getUserMedia audio in browser tiles. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- Camera in terminal programs (imagesnap, ffmpeg) and getUserMedia video in browser tiles. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.device.camera</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- navigator.geolocation in browser tiles (WebKit asks Core Location; Info.plist says why). -->
	<key>com.apple.security.personal-information.location</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
